 "about": {
  "text": "Ōtāhuhu A was the first power station on the Ōtāhuhu site in south Auckland, and the first large gas turbine station in Australia and New Zealand: four 45 MW open cycle gas turbines commissioned in 1968, joined by two more twin-pack units in 1978. The turbines burned fuel oil at first; the first was converted to natural gas from the Kapuni pipeline in 1971. It was returned to full operation during the 1992 power shortage. After it stopped generating in the late 1990s, its machines were kept running to supply reactive power to the national grid, until they were retired and decommissioned in 2013. Ōtāhuhu B, a combined cycle station, was built beside it.",
  "refs": [
   1,
   2,
   3
  ]
 },
